Control structures determine the order in which statements execute. Python has three: sequence, selection and repetition.
1. Sequence — statements run top to bottom.
   a = 5
   b = 3
   print(a + b)      # 8
2. Selection — choose a block by condition.
   if condition:
       block
   if-else:
   if marks >= 40:
       print("Pass")
   else:
       print("Fail")
   if-elif-else:
   if marks >= 80:
       print("A")
   elif marks >= 60:
       print("B")
   else:
       print("C")
   Nested if:
   if age >= 18:
       if citizen:
           print("Eligible to vote")
3. Repetition — repeat a block.
   for loop (known count):
   for i in range(1, 6):
       print(i)          # 1 2 3 4 5
   while loop (condition-controlled):
   n = 5
   while n > 0:
       print(n)
       n -= 1
   Nested loop:
   for i in range(1, 4):
       for j in range(1, 4):
           print(i, j)
Indentation (4 spaces) defines each block in Python.

a = int(input("Enter first number: "))
b = int(input("Enter second number: "))
print("1. Add  2. Subtract  3. Multiply  4. Divide")
choice = input("Enter your choice (1-4): ")
if choice == "1":
    print("Result =", a + b)
elif choice == "2":
    print("Result =", a - b)
elif choice == "3":
    print("Result =", a * b)
elif choice == "4":
    if b != 0:
        print("Result =", a / b)
    else:
        print("Error: division by zero is not allowed")
else:
    print("Invalid choice")

balance = 10000.00
pin = "1234"
attempts = 0
logged_in = False
print("========== WELCOME TO ATM ==========")
while attempts < 3 and not logged_in:
    entered = input("Enter PIN: ")
    if entered == pin:
        logged_in = True
    else:
        attempts += 1
        print("Wrong PIN!", 3 - attempts, "attempt(s) left.")
if not logged_in:
    print("Card blocked. Please contact your bank.")
else:
    print("Login Successful!")
    while True:
        print("-------- ATM MENU --------")
        print("1. Check Balance")
        print("2. Deposit")
        print("3. Withdraw")
        print("4. Exit")
        choice = input("Choose (1-4): ")
        if choice == "1":
            print(f"Your Balance: Rs. {balance:.2f}")
        elif choice == "2":
            amount = float(input("Enter deposit amount: Rs. "))
            balance += amount
            print(f"Deposited Rs. {amount:.2f}")
            print(f"New Balance: Rs. {balance:.2f}")
        elif choice == "3":
            amount = float(input("Enter withdrawal amount: Rs. "))
            if amount <= balance:
                balance -= amount
                print(f"Withdrawn Rs. {amount:.2f}")
                print(f"Remaining Balance: Rs. {balance:.2f}")
            else:
                print("Insufficient balance")
        elif choice == "4":
            print("Thank you! Goodbye.")
            break
        else:
            print("Invalid choice")

A DBMS is software that creates, stores, manages and secures databases and provides an interface between users/applications and the data. Its roles include:
• defining the database structure (tables, fields, keys, relationships);
• inserting, updating, deleting and retrieving data;
• enforcing integrity rules (primary keys, referential integrity, validation);
• controlling concurrent access so multiple users do not corrupt data;
• providing security through user accounts and permissions;
• backup and recovery after failures;
• supporting queries, forms and reports.
Examples: MS Access, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle.

Data are raw, unprocessed facts and figures without context: 42, 75, "Ali", 2026-10-25.
Information is data that has been processed, organized and interpreted so it has meaning: "Ali scored 75 out of 100 in 2026" or "average marks = 68%". Information supports decisions.
Example: the individual marks 60, 70, 80 are data; the average 70 and the statement "the class average is 70" are information.
A database is an organized collection of related data stored so that it can be accessed, managed and updated efficiently. It is usually organized into tables with rows (records) and columns (fields), with keys defining the relationships.
Advantages of a database:
• reduces data redundancy (data stored once, not in many files);
• maintains consistency (one update is seen everywhere);
• enforces integrity and validation rules;
• supports sharing by many users and applications;
• provides security, backup and recovery;
• allows powerful queries and reports;
• separates data from programs, so applications can change without rewriting the data.

Query 1 — selected fields: select StudentName, Class, Marks from Student; display only those columns.
Query 2 — AND filter: Class = "XI-A" AND Marks >= 60. Only students of XI-A who scored 60 or more are returned (both conditions must be true).
Query 3 — OR filter: Class = "XI-A" OR Class = "XI-B". Students from either class are returned (at least one condition is true).
SQL equivalents:
SELECT StudentName, Class, Marks FROM Student;
SELECT * FROM Student WHERE Class = 'XI-A' AND Marks >= 60;
SELECT * FROM Student WHERE Class = 'XI-A' OR Class = 'XI-B';
In Access Design View, enter the criteria in the Criteria row of each field; criteria on different fields on the same row are combined with AND, while criteria on separate rows are combined with OR.

Over-dependence can cause:
• loss of basic skills — inability to calculate, navigate or write without a device;
• health problems — eye strain, poor posture, reduced physical activity and sleep loss;
• social isolation — less face-to-face interaction and weaker communication skills;
• reduced attention and increased distraction from notifications and short videos;
• privacy and security risks from sharing too much data;
• vulnerability when systems fail — no power, no internet or a cyberattack stops work;
• job displacement and economic dependence on a few platforms.
Responsible use means balancing technology with offline activity, verifying information and keeping essential skills.

AI careers — machine-learning engineer, data scientist, computer-vision engineer, NLP engineer, AI product manager. In Pakistan, AI is used in fintech (fraud detection), telecom (customer support chatbots), agriculture (crop diagnosis) and healthcare imaging.
IoT careers — embedded-systems engineer, IoT solutions architect, firmware developer, network/automation engineer. Opportunities exist in smart agriculture, smart metering, industrial automation and home security.
Data Analytics careers — data analyst, business-intelligence analyst, database administrator, data engineer. Banks, telecoms, retailers, hospitals and government departments need analysts to turn data into decisions.
Assistive Technology careers — accessibility specialist, assistive-technology developer, rehabilitation engineer, UX designer for accessibility. There is growing demand in schools, hospitals and NGOs serving people with disabilities.
Skills to develop:
• programming (Python, JavaScript/C++), data structures and algorithms;
• mathematics and statistics for AI/analytics;
• databases and SQL, spreadsheets and data visualization;
• electronics and networking basics for IoT;
• cloud platforms, version control (Git) and cybersecurity awareness;
• communication, teamwork, problem-solving and English writing;
• domain knowledge (agriculture, health, education) and a portfolio of real projects.

Plagiarism is presenting another person's words, ideas, data, images or code as your own without acknowledgement, whether copied directly or paraphrased closely.
It should be avoided because:
• it is dishonest and misrepresents who did the work;
• it violates academic integrity rules and can lead to failing a subject or losing marks;
• it is an academic and ethical offence; if the copied work is protected by copyright, copying it without permission may also amount to copyright infringement (the two concepts are related but not identical);
• it stops the student from developing real understanding and skills;
• it damages the trust of teachers and future employers.
Avoid it by quoting and citing sources, keeping notes of where information came from, and writing in your own words.

Digital literacy is the ability to use digital tools and networks to find, evaluate, create, manage and communicate information responsibly and safely. It combines technical skills (using devices and software) with critical thinking (judging the reliability of information) and ethical behaviour (privacy, consent, citation, respectful communication).
It includes:
• finding information with effective search strategies;
• evaluating sources for authority, accuracy, currency and bias;
• creating content with word processors, spreadsheets, presentations and media tools;
• communicating and collaborating online;
• protecting privacy and security;
• using technology ethically and legally.
Why students need it:
1. Learning materials are increasingly online; students must find and judge them.
2. Assignments require research, data handling and digital presentation.
3. Misinformation spreads quickly; critical evaluation protects students from false claims.
4. Online safety — students must protect their data and recognize phishing and scams.
5. Future employment requires digital collaboration and productivity tools.
6. Responsible citizenship includes respectful online communication and respect for copyright.

